# ACT ONE

= Maggie keeps a lighthouse that no longer needs keeping.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE - LAMP ROOM - NIGHT #1#

The great lamp turns. Every few seconds its beam sweeps across MAGGIE (60s), wrapped in a coat two sizes too big, writing in a *logbook* by torchlight.

She checks the barometer, taps it, and writes again.

[[Should we see the date in the logbook?]]

MAGGIE (V.O.)
Fourteenth of November. Wind from the south-west, forty knots and rising. Glass falling. No ships.

She closes the book. Below, the sea **throws itself** against the rocks.

!SILENCE.

Then a RADIO crackles on the shelf.

RADIO (O.S.)
Low Tide Light, Low Tide Light, this is coastguard. Do you read?

Maggie lifts the handset.

MAGGIE
(into handset)
Coastguard, this is Low Tide. Reading you fine.

She waits. Nothing.

MAGGIE
Coastguard?

.THE STAIRWELL

Ninety-nine iron steps spiral down into the dark. Maggie takes them two at a time, which at her age is _a mistake_.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE - KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS

A kettle on a gas ring. A table for one, laid for two out of habit. TOM (40s), soaked to the skin, sits in the second chair as if he never left.

TOM
You still lay my place. Dad wrote to me, you know. Before. He said if anything happened to him you'd stay out here on your own until the sea took the whole place, and you with it, and he made me promise I'd come and talk you out of it. I said I would. I said it the way you say things to a man in a hospital bed, meaning it and not meaning it, and then I went back to the city and did nothing for three years.
(beat)
I'm sorry, Mags.

MAGGIE
You came because the ferry's off and the pub's shut.

TOM
That too.

Maggie sets two cups down. Hard.

MAGGIE
Sorry doesn't wind the lamp.

TOM ^
Neither do you, any more. It's automatic.

The kettle starts to ***whistle***.

MAGGIE
The lamp is. The logbook isn't. The weather isn't. Somebody has to write down what the sea does, Tom, or it might as well not have done it.

TOM
Who reads it?

MAGGIE
I do.

She pours. Steam rises between them.

CUT TO:

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE - GALLERY - LATER

Wind. Rain coming sideways. Brother and sister grip the rail.

        Far out, a single light blinks: red, then nothing, then red.

TOM
Is that a ship?

MAGGIE
(already moving)
Get the logbook.

/*
EXT. JETTY - NIGHT

An earlier version had them launch a boat. Too much.
*/

INT. LIGHTHOUSE - LAMP ROOM - MOMENTS LATER

Maggie at the radio. Tom holds the torch over the logbook.

MAGGIE
Coastguard, Low Tide. Vessel in distress, bearing two-seven-zero, three miles. Red flare.

@McCRAE (V.O.)
Low Tide, coastguard. Received. Lifeboat launching.

Maggie lets out a breath she has been holding for three years.

TOM
(quietly)
Somebody has to write it down.

She hands him the pen. In the margin he writes: \*see over\*.

~Low tide, low tide, keep it bright,
~somebody's coming home tonight.

MAGGIE
Dad used to sing that.

TOM
Badly.

MAGGIE
Very badly.

>FADE OUT.

===

